1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,850
Listen.

2
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:09,060
Practice.

3
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:09,920
Speak.

4
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,390
Practice makes you perfect.

5
00:00:13,950 --> 00:00:15,410
business tech upgrades.

6
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:22,530
We'll be talking about everything from CRM overhauls and network upgrades to the fascinating world of the Internet of Things.

7
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:26,470
And we've got a ton of real world business conversations to pull from.

8
00:00:26,690 --> 00:00:39,150
It's fascinating to see how these conversations, while focusing on different technologies, all seem to circle back to the same core challenges, data security, the need for effective user training, and of course, the ever-present hurdle of change management.

9
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:44,390
Yeah, implementing new technology isn't just about flipping a switch and expecting everything to magically work, right?

10
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:48,750
You really have to guide your entire organization through a transformation.

11
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:51,880
And I think these conversations really highlight that human element.

12
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:53,720
Let's dive into specifics, shall we?

13
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:55,460
Absolutely, let's.

14
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:58,830
Okay, let's start with something I know many of you are grappling with.

15
00:00:58,950 --> 00:00:59,990
CRM implementation.

16
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:03,390
One of the conversations we reviewed really hit home for me.

17
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:07,920
A sales manager was describing that sheer panic of switching over to a new CRM system.

18
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:13,390
All those worries about data migration, user adoption, potential system glitches.

19
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:15,530
It can feel really overwhelming.

20
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,160
Oh, it's definitely a common fear.

21
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:20,190
And that's why a phased rollout is often the best approach.

22
00:01:20,710 --> 00:01:23,250
One of the conversations actually offered a really practical tip.

23
00:01:24,010 --> 00:01:29,300
Start with a pilot group, maybe your sales team, to test the waters before launching company-wide.

24
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:35,330
That way you can work out the kinks and gather feedback and refine your training materials before going all in.

25
00:01:35,530 --> 00:01:36,830
That makes so much sense.

26
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:39,900
It's like dipping your toes in the pool before jumping in headfirst.

27
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:42,220
You get a feel for the temperature, so to speak.

28
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:50,580
And while we're talking about avoiding unpleasant surprises, let's talk about data security, which is absolutely paramount when it comes to CRMs.

29
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:51,320
Absolutely.

30
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:54,570
You're entrusting your valuable customer data to the system.

31
00:01:54,610 --> 00:02:00,210
So understanding the encryption protocols and involving your cybersecurity team from day one is crucial.

32
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:03,020
Don't just assume the vendor has it all covered.

33
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:06,980
Ask the tough questions and make sure your data is protected to the highest standards.

34
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:07,900
Exactly.

35
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,440
And it's not just about external threats.

36
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:10,870
It's about internal security as well.

37
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:12,790
Who has access to what data?

38
00:02:13,270 --> 00:02:16,790
How are you managing user permissions and preventing unauthorized access?

39
00:02:17,130 --> 00:02:19,660
These are all questions you need to be addressing from the outset.

40
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:21,760
And let's not forget about the integration piece.

41
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:28,180
You know, one of the conversations mentioned the frustration of having a CRM that doesn't play nicely with other software systems.

42
00:02:28,650 --> 00:02:30,450
You don't want to be creating data silos.

43
00:02:30,490 --> 00:02:34,390
You want a seamless flow of information across your entire organization.

44
00:02:34,530 --> 00:02:35,190
That's so true.

45
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:36,130
It's like building a house.

46
00:02:36,590 --> 00:02:39,980
You need all the rooms to connect in a logical way for it to function properly.

47
00:02:40,740 --> 00:02:46,750
And speaking of connections, let's move on to something that can really make or break productivity, especially for those remote teams.

48
00:02:47,730 --> 00:02:48,750
Network upgrades.

49
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:56,940
One of the conversations we reviewed talked about the pain of slow VPNs and the constant frustration of lagging network speeds.

50
00:02:56,970 --> 00:02:57,860
Does that sound familiar?

51
00:02:57,960 --> 00:02:58,660
Oh, absolutely.

52
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:02,220
I think we've all experienced that dreaded buffering wheel during a video call.

53
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:03,610
But what's the solution?

54
00:03:04,830 --> 00:03:08,060
One of the conversations mentioned SD-WAN as a potential game changer.

55
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:09,270
And it really can be.

56
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:14,000
Think of SD-WAN as a super smart traffic controller for your network.

57
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:18,880
It optimizes data flow so that those remote workers aren't stuck in a digital traffic jam.

58
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:23,190
It's like having a dedicated lane on the highway just for your business critical data.

59
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:24,310
I love that analogy.

60
00:03:24,710 --> 00:03:27,830
That makes such a complex concept so much easier to understand.

61
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:34,180
And what's really interesting is that SD-WAN, it not only speeds things up, but it also enhances security as well.

62
00:03:34,240 --> 00:03:34,900
Exactly.

63
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:42,160
It adds an extra layer of protection by creating secure tunnels for your data, reducing the risk of interception or unauthorized access.

64
00:03:42,470 --> 00:03:44,020
So we've got the speed and the security.

65
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:44,740
But let's be real.

66
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:47,420
Upgrading your network usually means new hardware.

67
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:50,670
And with new hardware comes the potential for, well, headaches.

68
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:57,140
I remember one conversation where a CIO was describing a network outage that brought their entire operation to a standstill.

69
00:03:57,460 --> 00:03:57,940
It was a nightmare.

70
00:03:58,080 --> 00:04:02,110
And that's why having a rock-solid contingency plan is essential.

71
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:08,950
Before you even start the upgrade process, ask yourself, can we quickly switch back to our old system if something goes wrong?

72
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:10,800
Do we have backups in place?

73
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:13,780
Are our employees prepared for potential disruptions?

74
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:15,200
Don't leave anything to chance.

75
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:16,560
That's such a great point.

76
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:22,970
It's so easy to get caught up in the excitement of new technology and completely forget about those potential pitfalls.

77
00:04:23,730 --> 00:04:26,790
Always have a plan B, especially when it comes to your network.

78
00:04:26,980 --> 00:04:27,320
All right.

79
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:33,930
Let's move on to something that has the potential to really revolutionize customer service, but also comes with its own set of challenges.

80
00:04:34,410 --> 00:04:35,970
AI powered chatbots.

81
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:36,830
Ah, yes.

82
00:04:36,890 --> 00:04:38,110
The rise of the chatbots.

83
00:04:38,890 --> 00:04:43,980
I'm all for efficiency, but I have to admit I've had some, to say, interesting encounters with some of those bots.

84
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:46,930
Sometimes it feels like they're speaking a completely different language.

85
00:04:46,990 --> 00:04:47,970
I think we've all been there.

86
00:04:48,370 --> 00:04:51,750
One of the conversations we reviewed actually talked about this very issue.

87
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:59,440
A customer service manager was expressing frustration with a chat bot that kept giving irrelevant answers and frustrating customers.

88
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:05,910
The takeaway, AI can be a powerful tool, but maintaining that human touch is crucial.

89
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:07,730
So how do we strike that balance?

90
00:05:08,190 --> 00:05:13,440
How do we harness the efficiency of AI without sacrificing that personal touch that customers crave?

91
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:28,220
Well, one of the conversations suggested using AI for those initial, often repetitive inquiries like password resets or order tracking, and then have a smooth escalation path to a human agent when things get more complex or, well, emotionally charged.

92
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:30,000
You know, nobody wants to pour their heart out to a bot.

93
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:30,480
No, right.

94
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:35,690
Sometimes you just need to talk to a real person who can empathize with your situation and offer a genuine solution.

95
00:05:36,230 --> 00:05:41,010
And this brings us back to data privacy, which is especially relevant when it comes to AI.

96
00:05:40,990 --> 00:05:46,280
These chatbots need data to learn, but that data needs to be, well, squeaky clean.

97
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:46,880
Absolutely.

98
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:49,880
Anonymized training data should be non-negotiable.

99
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:56,050
You are responsible for protecting that customer information, whether it's being used by a human or an algorithm.

100
00:05:56,070 --> 00:05:56,550
Okay.

101
00:05:56,570 --> 00:06:00,000
Let's shift gears and talk about something that often sends shivers down my spine.

102
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:01,700
Cybersecurity awareness.

103
00:06:02,190 --> 00:06:09,570
I remember one conversation where an IT manager was talking about finding a password sticky note stuck to the underside of someone's keyboard.

104
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:10,350
Yikes.

105
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:12,560
Oh, it's more common than we'd like to admit.

106
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:15,700
But that's exactly why employee training is paramount.

107
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:18,180
And one of the conversations offered a really interesting suggestion.

108
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:23,070
Take it beyond those, you know, boring click through training modules that everyone dreads.

109
00:06:23,090 --> 00:06:23,890
Resummification.

110
00:06:23,870 --> 00:06:24,330
Exactly.

111
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,780
Think leaderboards, rewards for completing training.

112
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:28,620
And this is a good one.

113
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:33,870
Regular phishing simulations to test employees' ability to spot those sneaky emails.

114
00:06:34,310 --> 00:06:38,540
Make security training engaging and relevant, not just a box to be ticked.

115
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:42,220
I've actually done those phishing simulations, and they are so eye-opening.

116
00:06:42,280 --> 00:06:44,660
It's amazing how sneaky some of those emails can be.

117
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:49,090
You think you're a savvy internet user, and then bam, you fall for a fake login page.

118
00:06:49,710 --> 00:06:50,210
It's humbling.

119
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:51,970
It's a constant arms race, isn't it?

120
00:06:52,370 --> 00:06:53,510
And it's not just about knowledge.

121
00:06:53,530 --> 00:06:54,570
It's about behavior change.

122
00:06:55,210 --> 00:06:58,160
And that's where the user experience of security software comes in.

123
00:06:58,660 --> 00:07:04,100
If your security software is clunky and frustrating to use, people will find ways to circumvent it.

124
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:04,820
Absolutely.

125
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,690
User-friendly security tools are crucial for compliance.

126
00:07:08,190 --> 00:07:10,830
If it's a pain to use, people will find workarounds.

127
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:12,990
And that's when those security gaps emerge.

128
00:07:13,470 --> 00:07:17,250
Let's move on to the big one, the one that keeps many business leaders up at night.

129
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:18,820
Cloud migration.

130
00:07:18,880 --> 00:07:23,400
Ah, yes, the cloud, that magical place where all our data lives happily ever after.

131
00:07:24,120 --> 00:07:26,600
But seriously, it's such a big decision for any business.

132
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:30,770
Full plunge into the cloud or dip a toe in with a hybrid approach.

133
00:07:31,510 --> 00:07:32,470
So many options.

134
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:42,260
The conversations we reviewed offer some great insights into this complex process, and the hybrid model seems to be gaining traction, offering that blend of flexibility and control

135
00:07:42,390 --> 00:07:45,070
So you're not putting all your eggs in one basket, so to speak.

136
00:07:45,790 --> 00:07:55,520
But one of the conversations I found really insightful was from a CIO who was talking about the challenges of integrating those legacy systems with a cloud environment.

137
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,130
Not everything plays well in the cloud sandbox.

138
00:07:58,330 --> 00:07:59,490
It's a key consideration.

139
00:07:59,550 --> 00:08:08,360
You need to carefully assess your existing systems and determine what can be migrated as is, what needs to be adapted, and what might need to be replaced altogether.

140
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:10,540
It's not a one-size-fits-all solution.

141
00:08:10,590 --> 00:08:17,610
While we're talking about potential pitfalls, let's not forget those critical aspects, data backup and disaster recovery in the cloud.

142
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:20,380
Those can make or break your migration.

143
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:21,000
Absolutely.

144
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:23,480
You can't just assume your data is safe because it's in the cloud.

145
00:08:24,140 --> 00:08:27,080
Regular testing of your disaster recovery plan is crucial.

146
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:30,430
Having redundancy built into your cloud infrastructure is key.

147
00:08:30,950 --> 00:08:32,450
What happens if there's an outage?

148
00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:33,370
Do you have a backup plan?

149
00:08:33,890 --> 00:08:37,250
These are questions you need to address before you even start migrating data.

150
00:08:37,490 --> 00:08:38,790
Redundancy is our friend.

151
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:42,560
Okay, ready to tackle another upgrade that can have a huge impact on a business.

152
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:46,380
The ERP system, out with the old, in with the new, right?

153
00:08:46,500 --> 00:08:50,900
Ah, yes, the ERP, the backbone of many businesses.

154
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:54,370
And the conversations we reviewed highlight a common wish list.

155
00:08:55,070 --> 00:09:00,170
Seamless integration, real-time analytics, and of course, mobile accessibility.

156
00:09:00,510 --> 00:09:03,440
It's all about having the right data at your fingertips wherever you are.

157
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:06,640
No more being chained to your desk to access critical information.

158
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:12,490
But one thing that struck me was that emphasis on seeking external guidance for these implementations.

159
00:09:12,670 --> 00:09:13,410
And it makes sense.

160
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:22,160
ERP implementations can be so incredibly complex, and having a seasoned consultant to guide you through that process can save you a lot of headaches down the road.

161
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,250
They've seen it all and can really help you avoid those common pitfalls.

162
00:09:25,450 --> 00:09:27,410
It's like having a Sherpa guide you up Mount Everest.

163
00:09:27,890 --> 00:09:31,780
They know the terrain, they've seen the dangers, and can help you reach the summit safely.

164
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:32,100
Right.

165
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:35,640
And the conversations also stress the importance of customization.

166
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:39,770
Your ERP should adapt to your unique workflows, not the other way around.

167
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:43,610
You don't want to force your employees to adapt to a system that just doesn't fit their needs.

168
00:09:44,010 --> 00:09:45,990
And of course, change management rears its head again.

169
00:09:46,890 --> 00:09:52,100
You've got to get the team on board early and make sure they understand how this new system will actually benefit them.

170
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,030
Otherwise, you're setting yourself up for resistance and frustration.

171
00:09:55,850 --> 00:10:00,730
Now, let's move on to a topic that is near and dear to my heart, data analytics.

172
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:05,350
We all know data is king, but it's useless if you can't make sense of it, right?

173
00:10:06,130 --> 00:10:07,050
Enter the BI tools.

174
00:10:07,610 --> 00:10:09,220
Yes, BI tools to the rescue.

175
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:13,380
The conversations mentioned the usual suspects, Tableau and Power BI.

176
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:19,130
But what I found really interesting was the potential impact of these tools on the roles within an analytics team.

177
00:10:19,710 --> 00:10:21,550
So it's not just about crunching numbers anymore.

178
00:10:21,830 --> 00:10:24,580
It's a shift from data gathering to data interpretation.

179
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:33,090
These BI tools automate much of the heavy lifting, freeing up analysts to focus on extracting insights and really telling the story behind the data.

180
00:10:33,210 --> 00:10:35,840
It's about turning data into actionable knowledge.

181
00:10:35,860 --> 00:10:39,620
And those insights need to be accessible to everyone, not just the data wizards, right?

182
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:46,750
One of the conversations talked about the importance of having dashboards that are easy to understand, even for people who aren't data experts.

183
00:10:46,990 --> 00:10:47,610
Absolutely.

184
00:10:47,650 --> 00:10:55,970
And the conversations also emphasize the importance of mobile BI, especially for executives who need to make those data driven decisions on the go.

185
00:10:56,570 --> 00:11:01,060
Imagine being able to pull up key performance indicators on your phone while you're waiting for a flight.

186
00:11:01,380 --> 00:11:03,120
That's the power of global BI.

187
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:05,330
It's like having a mini war room in your pocket.

188
00:11:05,730 --> 00:11:09,530
But with all this data flying around, data governance becomes paramount, right?

189
00:11:10,310 --> 00:11:17,570
One of the conversations mentioned the need for clear data dictionaries, consistent data definitions, and those robust access controls.

190
00:11:17,660 --> 00:11:24,610
We're talking security protocols, access policies, regular audits, all those things that ensure data integrity and compliance.

191
00:11:24,630 --> 00:11:26,020
It's not just about having the data.

192
00:11:26,100 --> 00:11:27,820
It's about managing it responsibly.

193
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:28,340
Okay.

194
00:11:28,680 --> 00:11:31,890
Ready to dive into a technology that's taking data to a whole new level?

195
00:11:32,410 --> 00:11:34,390
The Internet of Things, or IoT.

196
00:11:34,410 --> 00:11:34,930
Let's do it.

197
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:35,650
IoT.

198
00:11:36,050 --> 00:11:38,140
It's not just for your smart fridge anymore.

199
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:48,320
The conversations really paint this picture of real-time inventory tracking, predictive maintenance for equipment, even smart sensors that can monitor environmental conditions.

200
00:11:48,340 --> 00:11:50,180
The possibilities seem endless.

201
00:11:50,330 --> 00:11:55,400
What's fascinating is the discussion about where to process all that data, on the edge or in the cloud.

202
00:11:56,120 --> 00:12:02,420
Edge computing gives you those real-time insights, which is great for things like predictive maintenance, where you need to react quickly.

203
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:09,470
But cloud processing offers that more in-depth analytics, better for long-term trend analysis and strategic decision-making.

204
00:12:09,760 --> 00:12:12,020
So the choice really depends on your specific needs.

205
00:12:12,520 --> 00:12:14,300
No one-size-fits-all solution here.

206
00:12:14,940 --> 00:12:22,090
One conversation I found really helpful was from a manufacturing company that was using IoT sensors to monitor their production line in real time.

207
00:12:22,610 --> 00:12:28,680
They were able to identify bottlenecks, optimize their processes, and even prevent equipment failures before they happened.

208
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:29,520
It was amazing.

209
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:33,120
It's a great example of how IoT can drive real business value.

210
00:12:33,630 --> 00:12:38,530
But the conversations also highlight those big three for successful IoT implementation.

211
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:42,640
Security, scalability, and integration with existing systems.

212
00:12:42,660 --> 00:12:50,630
You've got to make sure your data is secure, that your system can handle the influx of data from all those sensors, and that your IoT platform can talk to your other business systems.

213
00:12:51,150 --> 00:12:52,690
And that idea of a pilot program.

214
00:12:52,950 --> 00:12:53,310
Brilliant.

215
00:12:53,980 --> 00:13:01,900
One of the conversations suggested starting with a small-scale deployment, maybe in a single department or facility, to test the waters before rolling it out company-wide.

216
00:13:02,520 --> 00:13:08,870
It's a great way to identify potential issues, refine your processes, and build confidence before making a larger investment.

217
00:13:09,310 --> 00:13:15,690
Now let's move on to a topic that's close to the heart of any organization that values continuous learning, the LMS.

218
00:13:15,790 --> 00:13:17,030
Time for a learning upgrade.

219
00:13:17,490 --> 00:13:20,110
The modern workforce demands a modern LMS.

220
00:13:20,650 --> 00:13:26,740
Out with those clunky, outdated systems and in with something that's engaging, accessible, and relevant to today's learners.

221
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:34,090
One of the conversations I found particularly insightful was from a learning and development manager who was talking about the need for mobile accessibility.

222
00:13:34,690 --> 00:13:39,640
Their employees were demanding training that they could access on their phones and tablets anytime, anywhere.

223
00:13:39,940 --> 00:13:40,580
Makes sense, right?

224
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:43,380
We live in a mobile world and learning should be no different.

225
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:45,280
But it's not just about mobile access.

226
00:13:45,370 --> 00:13:48,930
It's about creating a learning experience that's engaging and relevant.

227
00:13:48,950 --> 00:13:54,740
The conversations highlight the demand for interactive content, gamification, and personalized learning paths.

228
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:58,620
It's about meeting learners where they are, both physically and mentally.

